WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.427
In this lesson, Harrison will teach
the most important

2
00:00:08.427 --> 00:00:11.972
key building block of LangChain,
namely, the chain.

3
00:00:12.794 --> 00:00:15.687
The chain usually combines an LLM,
large language model,

4
00:00:15.687 --> 00:00:18.902
together with a prompt,
and with this building block you

5
00:00:18.902 --> 00:00:21.796
can also put a bunch of
these building blocks

6
00:00:21.796 --> 00:00:25.654
together to carry out a sequence
of operations on your text or

7
00:00:25.654 --> 00:00:26.940
on your other data.

8
00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:31.637
I'm excited to dive into it. Alright,
to start, we're going to load the

9
00:00:31.637 --> 00:00:33.650
environment variables as
we have before.

10
00:00:34.452 --> 00:00:37.996
And then we're
also going to load some data that we're going

11
00:00:37.996 --> 00:00:40.654
to use. So part
of the power of these

12
00:00:40.654 --> 00:00:43.608
chains is that you can
run them over many inputs

13
00:00:43.608 --> 00:00:46.857
at a time. Here we're going to
load a pandas DataFrame.

14
00:00:46.857 --> 00:00:46.857


15
00:00:47.378 --> 00:00:49.498
A pandas DataFrame is
just a data structure

16
00:00:49.498 --> 00:00:51.882
that contains a bunch
of different elements of data.

17
00:00:51.882 --> 00:00:54.532
If you're not familiar with pandas, don't
worry about it.

18
00:00:55.173 --> 00:00:57.518
The main point here is that we're
loading some data that we can then

19
00:00:58.099 --> 00:01:00.968
use later on. And so if we
look inside this pandas DataFrame,

20
00:01:00.968 --> 00:01:04.316
we can see that there is a product
column and then a review column.

21
00:01:04.316 --> 00:01:06.229
And each of these
rows is a different

22
00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:08.142
data point that we
can start passing through

23
00:01:08.142 --> 00:01:08.620
our chains.

24
00:01:09.963 --> 00:01:13.096
So the first chain we're
going to cover is the LLM chain. And

25
00:01:13.096 --> 00:01:15.025
this is a simple
but really powerful chain

26
00:01:15.025 --> 00:01:17.435
that underpins a lot
of the chains that we'll go

27
00:01:17.435 --> 00:01:18.400
over in the future.

28
00:01:18.901 --> 00:01:21.845
And so, we're going to import
three different things. We're going

29
00:01:21.845 --> 00:01:25.860
to import the OpenAI model, so the
LLM. We're going to import the chat prompt

30
00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.537
template. And so this
is the prompt. And then we're

31
00:01:28.537 --> 00:01:30.143
going to import
the LLM chain.

32
00:01:31.405 --> 00:01:34.238
And so first, what we're
going to do is we're going

33
00:01:34.238 --> 00:01:36.299
to initialize the language
model that we want

34
00:01:36.299 --> 00:01:38.359
to use. So we're going to
initialize the

35
00:01:38.981 --> 00:01:41.546
chat OpenAI with a high
temperature so that we can get

36
00:01:42.848 --> 00:01:44.051
some fun descriptions.

37
00:01:44.912 --> 00:01:47.084
Now we're
going to initialize a prompt. And

38
00:01:47.084 --> 00:01:50.613
this prompt is going to take in a
variable called product. It's going

39
00:01:50.613 --> 00:01:54.957
to ask the LLM to generate what the
best name is to describe a company that

40
00:01:54.957 --> 00:01:57.401
makes that product. And then
finally, we're going to

41
00:01:57.401 --> 00:01:59.301
combine these two things into
a chain.

42
00:02:00.022 --> 00:02:02.915
And so,
this is what we call an LLM chain.

43
00:02:02.915 --> 00:02:06.097
And it's quite simple. It's just
the combination of the LLM

44
00:02:06.097 --> 00:02:09.858
and the prompt. But now
this chain will let us run through the

45
00:02:09.858 --> 00:02:13.040
prompt and into the
LLM in a sequential manner. And so

46
00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:16.512
if we have a product called queen-size-sheet-set, we
can run this through

47
00:02:16.512 --> 00:02:17.958
the chain by using chain.run.


48
00:02:18.519 --> 00:02:20.843
And what this will,
do is it will format

49
00:02:20.843 --> 00:02:23.425
the prompt under the hood,
and then it will pass

50
00:02:23.425 --> 00:02:27.040
the whole prompt into the LLM.
And so we can see that we get

51
00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:29.106
back the name of
this hypothetical company called

52
00:02:29.106 --> 00:02:32.979
Royal Bettings. And so here would be
a good time to pause. And you can

53
00:02:32.979 --> 00:02:35.045
input any product
descriptions that you would want.

54
00:02:35.045 --> 00:02:38.144
And you can see what the
chain will output as a result.

55
00:02:38.144 --> 00:02:40.984
So the LLM chain is
the most basic type of chain.

56
00:02:41.625 --> 00:02:44.653
And that's
going to be used a lot in the future. And

57
00:02:44.653 --> 00:02:47.176
so we can see how
this will be used in

58
00:02:47.176 --> 00:02:49.952
the next type of chain, which
will be sequential chains. And

59
00:02:49.952 --> 00:02:51.970
so sequential chains run
a sequence of chains

60
00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:52.727
one after another.

61
00:02:53.308 --> 00:02:56.856
So to start, you're going to import the
simple sequential chain.

62
00:02:57.557 --> 00:02:59.641
And this works well
when we have subchains

63
00:02:59.641 --> 00:03:02.246
that expect only one
input and return only one output.

64
00:03:03.068 --> 00:03:05.372
And so here we're
going to first create one chain,

65
00:03:05.873 --> 00:03:06.855
which uses an LLM

66
00:03:07.617 --> 00:03:08.318
and a prompt.

67
00:03:08.879 --> 00:03:10.883
And this prompt is going to take in

68
00:03:11.765 --> 00:03:13.980
the product and will
return the best name

69
00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:17.025
to describe that company.
So that will be the first chain.

70
00:03:17.025 --> 00:03:19.240
Then we're
going to create a second chain.

71
00:03:19.821 --> 00:03:22.246
In this second chain, we'll
take in the company name

72
00:03:22.907 --> 00:03:26.855
and then output a 20-word
description of that company.

73
00:03:27.497 --> 00:03:30.410
And so you can imagine
how these chains might want

74
00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:34.198
to be run one after another,
where the output of the first chain,

75
00:03:34.198 --> 00:03:37.111
the company name,
is then passed into the second chain.

76
00:03:37.111 --> 00:03:39.733
We can easily do this by
creating a simple

77
00:03:39.733 --> 00:03:42.647
sequential chain where we have the
two chains described there.

78
00:03:43.368 --> 00:03:46.681
And we'll
call this overall simple chain. Now,

79
00:03:46.681 --> 00:03:48.338
what you can do

80
00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:52.246
is run this chain
over any product description.

81
00:03:53.248 --> 00:03:55.815
And so if we use
it with the product above,

82
00:03:55.815 --> 00:03:58.383
the queen size sheet set,
we can run it over

83
00:03:58.383 --> 00:04:00.950
and we can see that
it first outputs royal betting.

84
00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:03.004
And then it passes
it into the second

85
00:04:03.004 --> 00:04:05.058
chain and it comes
up with this description

86
00:04:05.058 --> 00:04:06.855
of what that company could be about.

87
00:04:08.699 --> 00:04:10.793
The simple sequential chain
works well when there's

88
00:04:10.793 --> 00:04:12.887
only a single input and a single output.


89
00:04:13.428 --> 00:04:16.514
But what about when there are
multiple inputs or multiple outputs?

90
00:04:16.514 --> 00:04:20.161
And so we can do this by using
just the regular sequential chain.

91
00:04:21.364 --> 00:04:24.252
So let's import that. And then you're going
to create a bunch of

92
00:04:24.252 --> 00:04:26.918
chains that we're going to
use one after another. We're going to

93
00:04:26.918 --> 00:04:29.139
be using the data from
above, which has a review.

94
00:04:29.139 --> 00:04:29.139


95
00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:31.584
And so the first chain, we're
going to take

96
00:04:32.125 --> 00:04:35.490
the review and translate it into
English.

97
00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:40.501
With the second chain, we're
going to create a summary of

98
00:04:40.501 --> 00:04:42.530
that review in one sentence.

99
00:04:44.294 --> 00:04:47.277
And this will use
the previously generated English

100
00:04:47.277 --> 00:04:47.650
review.

101
00:04:49.626 --> 00:04:51.995
The third chain is
going to detect what

102
00:04:51.995 --> 00:04:54.957
the language of the
review was in the first place.

103
00:04:54.957 --> 00:04:57.622
And so if you notice,
this is using the

104
00:04:57.622 --> 00:05:00.288
review variable that is
coming from the original review.

105
00:05:02.513 --> 00:05:05.479
And finally, the fourth chain will
take in multiple inputs.

106
00:05:05.479 --> 00:05:07.852
So this will take
in the summary variable,

107
00:05:07.852 --> 00:05:09.928
which we calculated with the second
chain,

108
00:05:10.549 --> 00:05:13.082
and the language variable,
which we calculated with the third

109
00:05:13.082 --> 00:05:13.335
chain.

110
00:05:13.876 --> 00:05:17.917
And it's going to ask for a follow-up response
to the summary in

111
00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:18.850
the specified language.

112
00:05:19.969 --> 00:05:22.154
One important thing to
note about all these subchains

113
00:05:22.715 --> 00:05:24.713
is that the input
keys and output keys

114
00:05:24.713 --> 00:05:25.962
need to be pretty precise.

115
00:05:26.804 --> 00:05:29.930
So here, we're taking in review. This
is a variable that will be

116
00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:31.133
passed in at the start.

117
00:05:31.874 --> 00:05:34.279
We can see that
we explicitly set the

118
00:05:34.279 --> 00:05:38.187
output key to English review.
This is then used in the next prompt,

119
00:05:38.187 --> 00:05:41.494
down below, where we take
in English review with that same

120
00:05:41.494 --> 00:05:42.096
variable name.

121
00:05:42.877 --> 00:05:45.142
And we set the output
key of that chain to summary,

122
00:05:45.703 --> 00:05:48.984
which we can see is
used in the final chain.

123
00:05:48.984 --> 00:05:51.936
The third prompt takes in review,
the original variable,

124
00:05:52.477 --> 00:05:53.700
and outputs language,

125
00:05:54.241 --> 00:05:55.965
which is again used in the final prompt.

126
00:05:57.107 --> 00:05:59.523
It's really important to get
these variable names lined

127
00:05:59.523 --> 00:06:02.207
up exactly right, because
there's so many different inputs and

128
00:06:02.207 --> 00:06:05.429
outputs going on. And if
you get any key errors, you should

129
00:06:05.429 --> 00:06:05.966
definitely check

130
00:06:06.467 --> 00:06:07.589
that they are lined up so.

131
00:06:08.952 --> 00:06:11.365
The simple sequential chain
takes in multiple chains,

132
00:06:11.365 --> 00:06:14.684
where each one has a
single input and a single output.

133
00:06:15.646 --> 00:06:18.532
To see a visual representation of this,
we can look at the slide,

134
00:06:19.193 --> 00:06:20.556
where it has one chain

135
00:06:21.358 --> 00:06:23.763
feeding into the other chain,
one after another.

136
00:06:25.186 --> 00:06:28.760
Here we can see a visual
description of the sequential chain.

137
00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:32.334
Comparing it to the above chain,
you can notice that any

138
00:06:32.334 --> 00:06:35.583
step in the chain can
take in multiple input variables.

139
00:06:35.583 --> 00:06:38.507
This is useful when you have
more complicated downstream

140
00:06:38.507 --> 00:06:41.431
chains that need to be a
composition of multiple

141
00:06:41.431 --> 00:06:42.081
previous chains.

142
00:06:43.624 --> 00:06:47.152
Now that we have all these chains,
we can easily combine

143
00:06:47.152 --> 00:06:50.679
them in the sequential chain. You'll
notice here that we'll pass

144
00:06:50.679 --> 00:06:53.245
in the four chains
we created into the

145
00:06:53.245 --> 00:06:56.451
chains variable. We'll create the
inputs variable with the one

146
00:06:56.451 --> 00:06:58.375
human input, which is the
review.

147
00:06:59.017 --> 00:07:02.442
And then we want to
return all the intermediate outputs.

148
00:07:02.442 --> 00:07:06.210
So the English review, the summary,
and then the follow-up message.

149
00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:11.206
Now,
we can run this over some of the data.

150
00:07:11.206 --> 00:07:16.050
So let's choose a review and pass it
in through the overall chain.

151
00:07:20.583 --> 00:07:23.309
We can see here
that the original review

152
00:07:23.309 --> 00:07:25.353
looks like it was in French.

153
00:07:25.974 --> 00:07:30.083
We can see the English review as a
translation. We can see a summary

154
00:07:30.083 --> 00:07:33.018
of that review and then
we can see a follow-up

155
00:07:33.018 --> 00:07:35.072
message in the original
language of French.

156
00:07:35.713 --> 00:07:37.944
You should pause the
video here and try

157
00:07:37.944 --> 00:07:39.060
putting in different inputs.

158
00:07:39.942 --> 00:07:43.368
So far we've covered the LLM chain and
then a sequential chain.

159
00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:46.435
But what if you want
to do something more complicated?

160
00:07:47.056 --> 00:07:49.369
A pretty common but
basic operation is to

161
00:07:49.369 --> 00:07:51.682
route an input to
a chain depending on

162
00:07:51.682 --> 00:07:53.128
what exactly that input is.

163
00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:58.167
A good way to imagine this is if
you have multiple sub chains,

164
00:07:58.167 --> 00:08:01.304
each of which specialized for
a particular type of input,

165
00:08:01.845 --> 00:08:03.886
you could have a
router chain which first

166
00:08:03.886 --> 00:08:06.946
decides which subchain to
pass it to and then passes it to

167
00:08:06.946 --> 00:08:07.456
that chain.

168
00:08:08.679 --> 00:08:11.948
For a concrete example, let's
look at where we

169
00:08:11.948 --> 00:08:14.854
are routing between different
types of chains depending

170
00:08:14.854 --> 00:08:18.123
on the subject that
seems to come in. So

171
00:08:18.123 --> 00:08:21.755
we have here different prompts. One
prompt is good for

172
00:08:21.755 --> 00:08:24.661
answering physics questions. The
second prompt is good

173
00:08:24.661 --> 00:08:27.931
for answering math questions, the
third for history, and

174
00:08:27.931 --> 00:08:30.837
then a fourth for computer science. Let's
define

175
00:08:30.837 --> 00:08:32.290
all these prompt templates.


176
00:08:35.211 --> 00:08:37.945
After we have these prompt templates,
we can then provide

177
00:08:37.945 --> 00:08:39.039
more information about them.

178
00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:42.326
We can give each one
a name and then a description.

179
00:08:43.007 --> 00:08:46.073
This description for
the physics one is good for

180
00:08:46.073 --> 00:08:49.139
answering questions about physics.
This information is going to

181
00:08:49.139 --> 00:08:52.886
be passed to the router chain,
so the router chain can

182
00:08:52.886 --> 00:08:54.930
decide when to use this subchain.

183
00:09:00.041 --> 00:09:02.826
Let's now import the other types
of chains that we need.

184
00:09:03.448 --> 00:09:05.071
Here we need a multi-prompt chain.

185
00:09:05.612 --> 00:09:09.148
This is a specific type of
chain that is used when routing

186
00:09:09.148 --> 00:09:10.622
between multiple different
prompt templates.

187
00:09:11.343 --> 00:09:14.488
As you can see, all the options we
have are prompt templates

188
00:09:14.488 --> 00:09:14.750
themselves.

189
00:09:15.411 --> 00:09:18.401
But this is just one type of thing
that you can route between.

190
00:09:18.401 --> 00:09:20.241
You can route between
any type of chain.

191
00:09:20.822 --> 00:09:23.317
The other classes that we'll
implement here are an

192
00:09:23.317 --> 00:09:24.149
LLM router chain.

193
00:09:24.710 --> 00:09:27.070
This uses a language
model itself to route

194
00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:29.430
between the different subchains.
This is where the

195
00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:32.084
description and the name
provided above will be used.

196
00:09:33.006 --> 00:09:35.110
We'll
also import a router output parser.

197
00:09:35.772 --> 00:09:38.663
This parses the LLM
output into a dictionary

198
00:09:38.663 --> 00:09:41.553
that can be used
downstream to determine which

199
00:09:41.553 --> 00:09:45.890
chain to use and what the input to
that chain should be.

200
00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:52.947
Now we can get around to using it.
First, let's import and define the language

201
00:09:52.947 --> 00:09:55.010
model that we will use.

202
00:09:56.858 --> 00:09:59.686
We now create the destination chains.
These are the chains

203
00:09:59.686 --> 00:10:01.948
that will be called
by the router chain.

204
00:10:02.449 --> 00:10:04.764
As you can see,
each destination chain itself

205
00:10:04.764 --> 00:10:07.079
is a language model chain,
an LLM chain.

206
00:10:09.203 --> 00:10:12.831
In addition to the destination chains,
we also need a default chain.

207
00:10:13.432 --> 00:10:17.075
This is the chain that's
called when the router can't decide

208
00:10:17.075 --> 00:10:21.049
which of the subchains to use. In the
example above, this might

209
00:10:21.049 --> 00:10:23.699
be called when the
input question has nothing

210
00:10:23.699 --> 00:10:26.679
to do with physics, math, history,
or computer science.

211
00:10:28.302 --> 00:10:32.179
Now we define the
template that is used by the LLM to

212
00:10:32.179 --> 00:10:33.794
route between the different chains.

213
00:10:34.836 --> 00:10:38.645
This has instructions of
the task to be done, as well as the

214
00:10:38.645 --> 00:10:40.989
specific formatting that the
output should be in.

215
00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:43.911
Let's put a few of
these pieces together to

216
00:10:43.911 --> 00:10:44.836
build the router chain.

217
00:10:45.758 --> 00:10:48.262
First, we create the full
router template by formatting

218
00:10:48.262 --> 00:10:50.488
it with the destinations
that we defined above.

219
00:10:51.009 --> 00:10:53.187
This template is flexible
to a bunch of

220
00:10:53.187 --> 00:10:54.276
different types of destinations.

221
00:10:54.917 --> 00:10:57.992
One thing you can do here is pause
and add different types

222
00:10:57.992 --> 00:10:58.505
of destinations.

223
00:10:59.106 --> 00:11:02.320
So up here, rather than just physics,
math, history, and computer science,

224
00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:04.998
you could add a different subject,
like English or Latin.

225
00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:08.924
Next, we create the prompt
template from this template,

226
00:11:08.924 --> 00:11:11.433
and then we create
the router chain by

227
00:11:11.433 --> 00:11:14.257
passing in the LLM
and the overall router prompt.

228
00:11:15.179 --> 00:11:18.475
Note that here we
have the router output parser.

229
00:11:18.475 --> 00:11:21.772
This is important as it will
help this chain

230
00:11:21.772 --> 00:11:23.970
decide which subchains
to route between.

231
00:11:25.961 --> 00:11:28.566
And finally, putting it all together,
we can create

232
00:11:29.107 --> 00:11:31.412
the overall chain.
This has a router chain,

233
00:11:32.134 --> 00:11:34.716
which is defined here.
It has destination chains,

234
00:11:34.716 --> 00:11:37.621
which we pass in here.
And then we also

235
00:11:37.621 --> 00:11:41.172
pass in the default chain.
We can now use this chain.

236
00:11:41.773 --> 00:11:43.116
So let's ask it some questions.

237
00:11:43.838 --> 00:11:47.517
If we ask it a question about physics,
we should hopefully see

238
00:11:47.517 --> 00:11:49.970
that it is routed
to the physics chain

239
00:11:50.832 --> 00:11:53.322
with the input,
what is blackbody radiation?

240
00:11:53.322 --> 00:11:56.879
And then that is passed
into the chain down below,

241
00:11:56.879 --> 00:12:00.437
and we can see that
the response is very detailed

242
00:12:00.437 --> 00:12:02.215
with lots of physics details.

243
00:12:03.137 --> 00:12:05.774
You should pause the
video here and try

244
00:12:05.774 --> 00:12:08.740
putting in different inputs.
You can try with all

245
00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:12.036
other types of special chains
that we have defined above.

246
00:12:12.717 --> 00:12:16.930
So, for example,
if we ask it a math question,

